Z||000000||Jncomutt||11-03-2001||01:34 PM||||Ok, I just got myself a set of the stainless steel brake lines.  When I took off the rubber hose from the caliper (driver side rear), no fluid leaked out.  I didn't think much of it and just continued to take off the old line.  I reconnected the steel line and tried to bleed the caliper.  I was by myself and using a mityvac.  I had the thing pumped up to about 25 and still no fluid was coming.   I gave the brake pedal a push and a bit of fluid came out, but still no action with the vac.  Tried for a little while, but nothing changed.<P>I noticed with the other calipers, when I bleed them using the pedal method, as soon as you crack the bleeder, the pedal goes right to the floor.  Doing this caliper, once you crack it open, it slowly goes down.<P>Is this the hard line thats bad???  The rear pass. side doesn't do it, just the driver side.  And if it is, where can I get just that hard line and is it Hard to replace??  <P>BTW, its for an 84, I noticed the hardline is different on my 86.||||reg||1
Z||000001||watts||11-03-2001||01:38 PM||||Seems like half the whole damn 84's are different in some little way...<P>Look for a pinched/crushed line somewhere.  If it's in an accesable spot, and the line's not rusty there, you can get an inline coupler for the appopriate size line.  Cut out the wrecked section, reflare both ends of the line and slip in the coupler.<P>Had to do that on my 4*4 a few times.  :/<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jncomutt||11-03-2001||02:20 PM||||Don't you need some sort of special tools to work on the hard lines or  to repair them??  And is there some sort of legal issue involved with like safety or something???||||reg||1
Z||000003||watts||11-03-2001||04:52 PM||||Well, it really depends on what you consider 'special' tools.  You need a line cutter, some emery cloth, and a double flare tool.  I've had all that stuff in my box for decades.  <P>Takes about 20 minutes to do the fix excluding bleeding the lines after.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||11-03-2001||08:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jncomutt:<BR><B>Don't you need some sort of special tools to work on the hard lines or  to repair them??  And is there some sort of legal issue involved with like safety or something???</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>some auto parts stores will loan or rent tools see if you can find one that will stock a flareing tool near you.<BR>some think if your rearend is at risk you will do a better job than a shop will.<BR>brakes need to be done right.<BR>see if your rear brake checkvalve is bad or parking brake part is frozen or cable stuck.<P><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-03-2001||11:06 PM||||Item One is physically following the line from end to end.<P>One of two things has heppened.<P>The line is crushed.<P>the line is plugged.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
